---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Sidebar from '../components/Sidebar.astro';
import Carousel from '../components/Carousel.astro';
import LogoWall from '../components/LogoWall.astro';
import ContactForm from '../components/ContactForm.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="Ambotics™" description="A new robotics company. Coming soon.">
  <main id="top" class="min-h-screen w-full">
    <div
      class="mx-auto max-w-[1440px] px-6 sm:px-10 lg:px-[84px] pt-16 sm:pt-20 lg:pt-[104px] pb-24"
    >
      <!-- Single grid: sticky left sidebar (logo + nav) + content -->
      <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[340px_minmax(0,1fr)]">
        <Sidebar />

        <!-- Content column -->
        <div class="min-w-0 mt-14 lg:mt-0">
          <h1
            class="font-display ss01 max-w-[360px] text-[clamp(28px,5vw,32px)] font-medium leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink"
          >
            A new kind of robotics company.
          </h1>

          <div class="mt-14 lg:mt-20">
            <section
              id="starting"
              class="max-w-content space-y-10 body-copy text-[14px] leading-[1.4] tracking-[-0.02em]"
            >
              <p>
                Robots are not new. They have been welding, painting, and lifting in
                factories since the 1960s, and they have been very good at it. The catch
                was always the conditions. A conventional robot needs the world held
                still around it — parts in fixed positions, a cage, and a program written
                for exactly one task. Change the task and you pay for the integration all
                over again. That is why robots filled car plants and stayed rare nearly
                everywhere else.
              </p>
              <p>
                AI is what changed. A robot can now see what is actually in front of it,
                handle parts that arrive at odd angles, and pick up a new task by being
                shown it instead of being reprogrammed. That sounds like a technical
                detail. It is not. It moves the line of what is worth automating. Small
                batches, frequent changeovers, and jobs that vary a little every time are
                now within reach.
              </p>
              <p>
                So the question stops being whether a machine can physically do the work.
                It becomes whether doing it that way pays. That is a business question,
                and most companies in this field are not built to answer it. They lead
                with the hardware and the breakthrough, and they speak to other engineers.
                We do the opposite. We lead with your business, in plain language, about
                your problem. If automating a task does not produce a real return for your
                operation, we will not take it on. That discipline is not a limitation. It
                is the whole promise.
              </p>
              <p>
                We own the entire stack: software, hardware, data, training, and model
                evaluation, so we can iterate faster than anyone else. When we do take
                something on, we own all of it. The hardware, the installation, the daily
                operation, and the support that keeps everything running long after the
                first day. You will never have to become a robotics expert or assemble a
                line of vendors to make it work. You show us where the repetitive work
                lives. We carry the rest.
              </p>
            </section>

            <LogoWall />

            <Carousel />

            <section
              class="mt-24 max-w-content space-y-10 body-copy text-[14px] leading-[1.4] tracking-[-0.02em]"
            >
              <p>
                Most of our customers start in the same place. They have a sense that
                something in their operation could be done better, and they are not sure
                what is possible or where to begin. That is reasonable. The line moved
                recently, and it is not obvious from the outside where it now sits.
              </p>
              <p>
                Tell us where the repetitive work lives. We will tell you plainly whether
                there is a case for automating it. If there is, we will build it, run it,
                and stand behind it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <ContactForm />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
